#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# squares_r

#% text
# `squares_r(d, k)` gives the number of ways to write the integer `k` as a sum of `d` squares of integers, counting order and signs.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For `d` = 2, 4, 6, 8 the classical divisor-sum formulas are used (any `k`); other `d` use convolution of the one-square counts (`k` up to 3000).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
squares_r(2, 45)
#% output
#= 8

#% cell
squares_r(4, 20)
#% output
#= 144

#% cell
squares_r(2, [1, 2, 5, 25])
#% output
#= [4, 4, 8, 12]

#% cell
sum(squares_r(2, k), [k, 0, 20^2])
#% output
#= 1257

#% section
# See also

#% text
